hi my name is anish and I'm a junior  here at MIT and I'm working on building  provably correct peer-to-peer systems so  it turns out that peer-to-peer is an  incredibly useful paradigm for building  systems like for example fit point is a  peer-to-peer system and there are a  whole lot of things built in a  peer-to-peer manner um it turns out that  writing software is hard people make  mistakes and buggy systems lead to  problems so how can we be sure that our  peer-to-peer systems are correct because  they're so important right if something  happens bitcoin breaks and people lose  money that would be terrible so how do  we know that our software is correct  well we can use tests and we can write a  bunch of tests and check in without  prepares but that's not really great  because it doesn't cover all the corner  cases and we can't be super sure that  our code is pressed we can try proving  properties about our code on paper but  that isn't great either because it just  proves properties about the protocol and  it didn't prove that our implementation  is correct and so the way to solve this  problem is by using formal verification  and what that is is it's implementing  code in such a way that's it the  implementation of a program can be  checked against with specification in  such a way that the computer can  basically verify that it is correct and  so it provides an end-to-end guarantee  that a piece of code implements a  specification and I'm applying this  technique to peer-to-peer systems
 

